      {
        "path": "Vec",
        "kind": "struct",
        "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/vec/struct.Vec.html",
        "docs": [
          {
            "type": "truncatedBlock",
            "nodes": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "A contiguous growable array type, written as ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec<T>",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", short for 'vector'.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Title",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Examples",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "let mut vec = Vec::new();\nvec.push(1);\nvec.push(2);\n\nassert_eq!(vec.len(), 2);\nassert_eq!(vec[0], 1);\n\nassert_eq!(vec.pop(), Some(2));\nassert_eq!(vec.len(), 1);\n\nvec[0] = 7;\nassert_eq!(vec[0], 7);\n\nvec.extend([1, 2, 3]);\n\nfor x in &vec {\n    println!(\"{x}\");\n}\nassert_eq!(vec, [7, 1, 2, 3]);"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "The ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "vec!",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/macro.vec.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " macro is provided for convenient initialization:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "let mut vec1 = vec![1, 2, 3];\nvec1.push(4);\nlet vec2 = Vec::from([1, 2, 3, 4]);\nassert_eq!(vec1, vec2);"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "It can also initialize each element of a ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec<T>",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " with a given value.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "This may be more efficient than performing allocation and initialization",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "in separate steps, especially when initializing a vector of zeros:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "let vec = vec![0; 5];\nassert_eq!(vec, [0, 0, 0, 0, 0]);\n\n// The following is equivalent, but potentially slower:\nlet mut vec = Vec::with_capacity(5);\nvec.resize(5, 0);\nassert_eq!(vec, [0, 0, 0, 0, 0]);"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "For more information, see",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Capacity and Reallocation",
                    "style": "Plain",
                    "url": "#capacity-and-reallocation"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Use a ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec<T>",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " as an efficient stack:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "let mut stack = Vec::new();\n\nstack.push(1);\nstack.push(2);\nstack.push(3);\n\nwhile let Some(top) = stack.pop() {\n    // Prints 3, 2, 1\n    println!(\"{top}\");\n}"
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Title",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Indexing",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "The ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " type allows access to values by index, because it implements the",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Index",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/ops/index/index.html?search=core%3A%3Aops%3A%3Aindex%3A%3AIndex"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " trait. An example will be more explicit:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "let v = vec![0, 2, 4, 6];\nprintln!(\"{}\", v[1]); // it will display '2'"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "However be careful: if you try to access an index which isn't in the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ",",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "your software will panic! You cannot do this:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "let v = vec![0, 2, 4, 6];\nprintln!(\"{}\", v[6]); // it will panic!"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Use ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "get",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://docs.rs/slice/latest/slice/index.html?search=slice%3A%3Aget"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "get_mut",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://docs.rs/slice/latest/slice/index.html?search=slice%3A%3Aget%5Fmut"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " if you want to check whether the index is in",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Title",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Slicing",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "A ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " can be mutable. On the other hand, slices are read-only objects.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "To get a ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "slice",
                    "style": "Plain",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/index.html?search=core%3A%3Aslice"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", use ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "&",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/index.html?search=core%3A%3Areference"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". Example:",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "codeBlock",
                "lang": "rust",
                "code": "fn read_slice(slice: &[usize]) {\n    // ...\n}\n\nlet v = vec![0, 1];\nread_slice(&v);\n\n// ... and that's all!\n// you can also do it like this:\nlet u: &[usize] = &v;\n// or like this:\nlet u: &[_] = &v;"
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "In Rust, it's more common to pass slices as arguments rather than vectors",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "when you just want to provide read access. The same goes for ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "String",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/string/struct.String.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "&str",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/index.html?search=core%3A%3Astr"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Title",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Capacity and reallocation",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "The capacity of a vector is the amount of space allocated for any future",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "elements that will be added onto the vector. This is not to be confused with",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "length",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " of a vector, which specifies the number of actual elements",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "within the vector. If a vector's length exceeds its capacity, its capacity",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "will automatically be increased, but its elements will have to be",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "reallocated.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "For example, a vector with capacity 10 and length 0 would be an empty vector",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "with space for 10 more elements. Pushing 10 or fewer elements onto the",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "vector will not change its capacity or cause reallocation to occur. However,",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "if the vector's length is increased to 11, it will have to reallocate, which",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "can be slow. For this reason, it is recommended to use ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec::with_capacity",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/vec/struct.Vec.html#method.with_capacity"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "whenever possible to specify how big the vector is expected to get.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": "Title",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Guarantees",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Due to its incredibly fundamental nature, ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " makes a lot of guarantees",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "about its design. This ensures that it's as low-overhead as possible in",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "the general case, and can be correctly manipulated in primitive ways",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "by unsafe code. Note that these guarantees refer to an unqualified ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec<T>",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ".",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "If additional type parameters are added (e.g., to support custom allocators),",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "overriding their defaults may change the behavior.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "Most fundamentally, ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is and always will be a (pointer, capacity, length)",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "triplet. No more, no less. The order of these fields is completely",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "unspecified, and you should use the appropriate methods to modify these.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "The pointer will never be null, so this type is null-pointer-optimized.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "However, the pointer might not actually point to allocated memory. In particular,",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "if you construct a ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " with capacity 0 via ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec::new",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/vec/struct.Vec.html#method.new"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "vec![]",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/macro.vec.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ",",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec::with_capacity(0)",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/vec/struct.Vec.html#method.with_capacity"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", or by calling ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "shrink_to_fit",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/vec/struct.Vec.html#method.shrink_to_fit"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "on an empty Vec, it will not allocate memory. Similarly, if you store zero-sized",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "types inside a ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", it will not allocate space for them. ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Note that in this case",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "the ",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " might not report a ",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "capacity",
                    "style": "InlineCode",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/vec/struct.Vec.html#method.capacity"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " of 0",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " will allocate if and only",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "if ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "size_of::",
                    "style": "Plain",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/mem/index.html?search=core%3A%3Amem%3A%3Asize%5Fof"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "<T>",
                    "style": "Plain",
                    "url": "https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/mem/index.html?search=core%3A%3Amem%3A%3Asize%5Fof"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "() * ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "capacity",
                    "style": "Plain",
                    "url": "http://docs.rust-lang.org/nightly/alloc/vec/struct.Vec.html#method.capacity"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "() > 0",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". In general, ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "'s allocation",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "details are very subtle --- if you intend to allocate memory using a ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "and use it for something else (either to pass to unsafe code, or to build your",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "own memory-backed collection), be sure to deallocate this memory by using",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "from_raw_parts",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " to recover the ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and then dropping it.",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "spans": [
                  {
                    "text": "If a ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Vec",
                    "style": "InlineCode"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "has",
                    "style": "Emphasis"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " allocated memory, then the memory it points to is on the heap",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "(as defined by the allocator Rust is configured to use by default), and its",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "style": "Plain"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "pointer points to ",
